.TH FMA 3 "May 2026" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
fma, fmaf, fmal \- fused multiply-add
.SH LIBRARY
Math library (libm, \-lm)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <math.h>
.PP
.BI "double fma(double " x ", double " y ", double " z );
.br
.BI "float fmaf(float " x ", float " y ", float " z );
.br
.BI "long double fmal(long double " x ", long double " y ", long double " z );
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR fma ()
function computes
.IR "x * y + z "
with only a single rounding operation.  This differs from the naive
sequence
.IR "x * y + z" ,
which involves two separate roundings: one after the multiplication and
another after the addition.
.PP
The fused multiply-add operation is particularly useful for:
.IP \(bu 3
Dot products of vectors, where the accumulation of many products
requires high precision.
.IP \(bu 3
Polynomial evaluation (Horner's method).
.IP \(bu 3
Compensated summation and other numerically sensitive algorithms.
.PP
By reducing rounding error from two operations to one, the FMA operation
provides more accurate results for many numerical computations.
.PP
On hardware that supports a native FMA instruction (such as modern x86_64,
ARM, or POWER processors), the function is typically implemented directly.
On platforms without native FMA support (such as x87-only i386 systems),
the implementation uses Dekker's algorithm or similar techniques to achieve
the single-rounding semantics in software.
.PP
The
.BR fmaf ()
and
.BR fmal ()
variants take and return
.B float
and
.B "long double"
values respectively.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success, these functions return
.I "(x * y) + z"
computed with a single rounding operation.
.PP
If the product
.IR "x * y"
overflows but the result of adding
.I z
is representable, the result is rounded once and returned.
.PP
If
.I x
or
.I y
is zero and the other is infinity, or if
.I x
or
.I y
is infinity and the other is zero, the special case 0 × ∞ is treated as a
domain error, returning NaN and raising
.BR FE_INVALID .
.PP
If the mathematically exact result overflows the range of the return type,
.B HUGE_VAL
(or
.B HUGE_VALF
or
.BR HUGE_VALL )
is returned with the appropriate sign, and an overflow exception
.RB ( FE_OVERFLOW )
is raised.
.PP
If the mathematically exact result underflows to zero, zero with the
appropriate sign is returned, and an underflow exception
.RB ( FE_UNDERFLOW )
is raised.
.PP
If any argument is NaN, NaN is returned.
.PP
If
.I x
and
.I y
are infinite but with opposite signs (e.g., +∞ and −∞), the result
depends on the value of
.IR z :
if
.I z
is also infinite, the sign of the infinities determines whether the result
is +∞, −∞, or NaN.
.SH ERRORS
See
.BR math_error (7)
for information on how to determine whether an error has occurred when
calling these functions.
.PP
The following errors can occur:
.TP
Domain error: \fIx\fR × \fIy\fR is 0 × ∞
An invalid floating-point exception
.RB ( FE_INVALID )
is raised.  If
.I "math_errhandling & MATH_ERRNO"
is non-zero,
.I errno
is set to
.BR EDOM .
.TP
Range error: overflow
An overflow floating-point exception
.RB ( FE_OVERFLOW )
is raised.  If
.I "math_errhandling & MATH_ERRNO"
is non-zero,
.I errno
is set to
.BR ERANGE .
.TP
Range error: underflow
An underflow floating-point exception
.RB ( FE_UNDERFLOW )
is raised.  If
.I "math_errhandling & MATH_ERRNO"
is non-zero,
.I errno
is set to
.BR ERANGE .
.SH ATTRIBUTES
For an explanation of the terms used in this section, see
.BR attributes (7).
.TS
allbox;
lb lb lb
l l l.
Interface	Attribute	Value
T{
.BR fma (),
.BR fmaf (),
.BR fmal ()
T}	Thread safety	MT-Safe
.TE
.SH STANDARDS
.BR fma ()
and its variants conform to C99 and POSIX.1-2008.
.PP
The handling of special arguments follows C99 Annex F (IEC 60559).
.SH EXAMPLES
The following program demonstrates computing a dot product with high
precision using
.BR fma ():
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <math.h>
#include <stdio.h>

double
dot_product(const double *a, const double *b, int n)
{
    double sum = 0.0;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
        sum = fma(a[i], b[i], sum);
    return sum;
}

int
main(void)
{
    double x[] = { 1.0, 2.0, 3.0 };
    double y[] = { 4.0, 5.0, 6.0 };

    printf("Dot product: %.1f\en", dot_product(x, y, 3));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
The output is:
.PP
.RS 4
Dot product: 32.0
.RE
